---
title: "South Africa's local election puts everyday services in the spotlight"
summary: "The ANC remains the largest party by national vote share, but the local results point to a more fragmented map of municipal power."
category: Politics
date: 2026-11-13
author: "Fahamu News Desk"
---

South Africa's 4 November local elections turned attention to the level of government people encounter most directly: the municipality. These councils oversee local services and infrastructure, from water and roads to waste collection.

The Electoral Commission announced the final results on 8 November. The African National Congress (ANC) won 30.76% of the vote and 3,246 council seats. The Democratic Alliance received 24.64%, while other parties and independent candidates shared a substantial part of the vote. National turnout was 52%, up from 45.9% in the 2021 local elections.

## A bigger field, more negotiation

The ANC remained the largest party by national vote share, but its result was far below its previous local-election support. The Associated Press described service failures, including water and electricity outages and uncollected rubbish, as major concerns raised during the campaign. That gives the result a local dimension: voters were choosing who should manage services in their towns and cities.

The vote was also spread across a large field. More than 500 parties and hundreds of independent candidates contested the elections, according to the Electoral Commission. In municipalities where no party has a majority, coalition negotiations will help determine who forms a council and which priorities make it into local budgets.

## The work starts after election day

A national percentage is only one part of the story. Municipal outcomes vary from place to place, and the daily test for elected councillors is whether residents see reliable services and clear accountability. The Electoral Commission says turnout rose, while the final balance of parties means many local decisions will require cooperation.

For voters, the next question is concrete: will a new council improve the basics residents depend on, and can residents track whether it does?

**Sources:** [South African Government News Agency on final results](https://www.sanews.gov.za/node/85517) · [Associated Press on the election and service issues](https://apnews.com/article/elections-south-africa-anc-services-johannesburg-35f673cfe16bee26a94f9810d221771f)
